1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,350
So let's go back to Isaac Newton.

2
00:00:04,974 --> 00:00:09,450
It's the start of 1666, a hundred years
before Dalton is born.

3
00:00:10,168 --> 00:00:14,460
At least, that's the date Newton gives
when he writes it up, six years later.

4
00:00:15,448 --> 00:00:16,720
He's 23.

5
00:00:17,348 --> 00:00:20,250
He has just bought a triangular glass
prism.

6
00:00:20,881 --> 00:00:23,990
People already know a prism can throw
colours on a wall.

7
00:00:24,901 --> 00:00:29,870
Newton wants to know where those colours
come from, so he darkens his room.

8
00:00:30,595 --> 00:00:33,165
He makes a small hole in his window
shutter,

9
00:00:33,215 --> 00:00:36,210
and he lets one thin beam of sunlight in.

10
00:00:37,215 --> 00:00:39,130
He puts the prism in the beam.

11
00:00:39,809 --> 00:00:44,830
The glass bends the light and on the far
wall, a band of colours appears.

12
00:00:45,443 --> 00:00:49,380
Red at one end, violet at the other, and
everything in between.

13
00:00:50,243 --> 00:00:52,980
The first thing that surprises him is its
shape.

14
00:00:53,676 --> 00:00:56,440
The beam came in through a small round
hole,

15
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:01,140
but the band on the wall is long, about
five times longer than it is wide.

16
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:05,950
By the rules he'd been taught, he says, it
should have been round.

17
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:10,410
At the time, most people who thought about
it believed something simple.

18
00:01:11,204 --> 00:01:12,790
White light is pure.

19
00:01:13,498 --> 00:01:16,810
The prism changes it, and that's what
makes the colours.

20
00:01:17,698 --> 00:01:20,080
Newton suspects it's the other way round.

21
00:01:20,871 --> 00:01:24,941
So he sets up a test he will later call
the Experimentum Crucis,

22
00:01:24,991 --> 00:01:26,490
the crucial experiment.

23
00:01:27,451 --> 00:01:31,070
He lets the band of colours fall on a
board with a small hole in it.

24
00:01:31,865 --> 00:01:35,020
Only one colour gets through, say, just
the blue.

25
00:01:35,739 --> 00:01:38,520
Then he sends that blue light through a
second prism.

26
00:01:39,379 --> 00:01:40,929
If the glass makes the colours,

27
00:01:40,979 --> 00:01:44,030
the second prism should turn that blue
into something else.

28
00:01:44,773 --> 00:01:45,773
It doesn't.

29
00:01:46,426 --> 00:01:49,530
The blue bends again, by its own fixed
amount.

30
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:51,950
He tries the other colours too.

31
00:01:52,734 --> 00:01:57,324
Once a colour is separated, he says, it
"obstinately retained its colour".

32
00:01:57,374 --> 00:01:58,850
whatever he did to it.

33
00:01:59,854 --> 00:02:03,350
So the prism isn't making the colours,
it's sorting them.

34
00:02:04,148 --> 00:02:08,170
Every kind of ray was in the white
sunlight all along, mixed together.

35
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,280
The glass just spreads them out.

36
00:02:12,281 --> 00:02:13,880
He checks it the other way too.

37
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:17,650
He gathers the spread-out rays back
together with a lens.

38
00:02:18,349 --> 00:02:20,490
Where they meet, they make white again.

39
00:02:21,263 --> 00:02:25,520
In his words, light "entirely and
perfectly white".

40
00:02:26,383 --> 00:02:30,630
Ever seen a little rainbow thrown on a
wall by a window, or a glass of water?

41
00:02:31,356 --> 00:02:33,880
Then you've seen the first half of his
experiment.

42
00:02:34,796 --> 00:02:37,220
In his letter, he puts it like this.

43
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:42,140
"Colours are not Qualifications of light
derived from Refractions,

44
00:02:42,190 --> 00:02:44,630
or Reflections of natural Bodies."

45
00:02:45,464 --> 00:02:48,340
Put simply, glass doesn't create colour.

46
00:02:49,078 --> 00:02:51,720
Light bouncing off things doesn't create
it either.

47
00:02:52,491 --> 00:02:57,550
Each kind of ray carries something fixed,
and that decides which colour you'll see.

48
00:02:58,551 --> 00:03:03,110
He sends all this in a letter from
Cambridge, in February 1672.

49
00:03:03,825 --> 00:03:06,355
It's printed in the Philosophical
Transactions,

50
00:03:06,405 --> 00:03:09,140
the journal run by the Royal Society's
secretary.

51
00:03:10,025 --> 00:03:13,240
Plenty of readers like it, but not all of
them.

52
00:03:14,039 --> 00:03:17,740
Robert Hooke, a leading London scientist,
is not impressed.

53
00:03:18,239 --> 00:03:19,420
And says so.

54
00:03:20,493 --> 00:03:25,230
The arguments, with Hooke and others, drag
on by letter for about six years.

55
00:03:26,093 --> 00:03:28,100
But notice what Newton has done.

56
00:03:28,786 --> 00:03:31,130
He's taken colour out of the glass.

57
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:33,000
So where is it?
